Let's craft final answer.

### TL;DR  
Layers are painted **in the order they appear in the style’s layer list**.  
When you call `map.addLayer()` without a second argument the new layer is appended to the end – that’s why it ends up **on top of the street‑name/label symbols**.  

Fix it by **inserting the layer before the first label layer** (or by moving it later with `map.moveLayer`).  

Below is a complete, copy‑and‑paste ready recipe that works for both **Mapbox GL JS** and **MapLibre GL JS**.

---

## 1. Find the label layer(s) you want to stay on top

The built‑in “Mapbox Streets” style (or any other vector‑tile style) contains a bunch of symbol layers whose `type` is `"symbol"` and whose `layout.text-field` is set. Those are the layers that draw street names, place names, POI labels, etc.

```js
// Helper: log all symbol layers (labels) in the current style
function logLabelLayers() {
  const labelLayers = map.getStyle().layers.filter(l => l.type === 'symbol');
  console.log('Label layers (first to last):');
  labelLayers.forEach(l => console.log(l.id));
}
map.on('load', logLabelLayers);
```

Typical IDs you’ll see (order may vary by style version):

```
road-label
road-number-shield
waterway-label
poi-label
place-label
```

Pick the **first** label layer you want your data to appear *under*.  
If you just want to stay under *all* labels, use the **first** symbol layer in the list (often `road-label`).

---

## 2. Add your GeoJSON source & layer **before** that label layer

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // 1️⃣ Add the source (only once)
  map.addSource('my-data', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: myGeojsonObject   // ← your GeoJSON here
  });

  // 2️⃣ Define the visual style of the layer
  const myLayer = {
    id: 'my-data-layer',
    type: 'fill',               // or 'line', 'circle', etc.
    source: 'my-data',
    paint: {
      'fill-color': '#ff6600',
      'fill-opacity': 0.4
    }
  };

  // 3️⃣ Insert it *before* the first label layer
  const firstLabelId = 'road-label';   // <-- change if your style uses a different ID
  map.addLayer(myLayer, firstLabelId);
});
```

**What happens?**  
`map.addLayer(layer, beforeId)` tells the renderer: *“Insert this layer right before the layer whose id is `beforeId`.”*  
Because `road-label` (or whichever label you chose) stays **above** your new layer, the labels will be drawn on top of your polygons/lines/points.

---

## 3. If you already added the layer (or you don’t know the label ID yet)

You can always **re‑order** later with `moveLayer`:

```js
// Move an existing layer to a new position
// Syntax: map.moveLayer(layerId, beforeId);
map.moveLayer('my-data-layer', 'road-label');
```

Or, if you want to push it *behind* **all** symbol layers:

```js
// Find the first symbol layer dynamically
const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol');
if (firstSymbol) {
  map.moveLayer('my-data-layer', firstSymbol.id);
}
```

---

## 4. Full “copy‑paste” example (Mapbox GL JS v2 / MapLibre)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>GeoJSON under labels</title>
  <script src="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/v2.15.0/mapbox-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/v2.15.0/mapbox-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  mapboxgl.accessToken = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN';

  const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12', // any style you like
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12
  });

  // Your GeoJSON (replace with your own)
  const myGeojsonObject = {
    "type": "FeatureCollection",
    "features": [
      {
        "type": "Feature",
        "properties": {},
        "geometry": {
          "type": "Polygon",
          "coordinates": [[
            [-122.452, 37.75],
            [-122.452, 37.76],
            [-122.44, 37.76],
            [-122.44, 37.75],
            [-122.452, 37.75]
          ]]
        }
      }
    ]
  };

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Add source
    map.addSource('my-data', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: myGeojsonObject
    });

    // 2️⃣ Add layer *before* the first label layer
    const firstLabelId = 'road-label'; // change if needed
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'my-data-layer',
      type: 'fill',
      source: 'my-data',
      paint: {
        'fill-color': '#ff6600',
        'fill-opacity': 0.4
      }
    }, firstLabelId);
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## 5. Quick checklist

| ✅ What to do | ✅ How |
|---------------|-------|
| **Identify the label layer** you want on top | `map.getStyle().layers.filter(l => l.type === 'symbol')` |
| **Add your layer before it** | `map.addLayer(myLayer, labelId)` |
| **Re‑order after the fact** | `map.moveLayer('my-layer-id', labelId)` |
| **Keep it under *all* labels** | Use the **first** symbol layer (`road-label` in most Mapbox Streets styles) |
| **If you’re using a custom style** | Open the style JSON, look for layers with `"type": "symbol"` and note their IDs |

---

### Bonus: Keep your code robust across style versions

If you ever switch to a different style (e.g., `satellite-streets`, a custom style, or a future version of Mapbox Streets), hard‑coding `"road-label"` might break. The following helper automatically finds the *first* symbol layer, guaranteeing that your data always stays under the labels regardless of the style:

```js
function addLayerUnderLabels(layerDef) {
  const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol');
  const beforeId = firstSymbol ? firstSymbol.id : undefined; // undefined = append (fallback)
  map.addLayer(layerDef, beforeId);
}

// usage
addLayerUnderLabels({
  id: 'my-data-layer',
  type: 'fill',
  source: 'my-data',
  paint: { 'fill-color': '#ff6600', 'fill-opacity': 0.4 }
});
```

---

## TL;DR Code Snippet

```js
// After the style has loaded …
map.on('load', () => {
  // 1️⃣ source
  map.addSource('my-data', {type: 'geojson', data: myGeojson});

  // 2️⃣ find the first label (symbol) layer
  const firstLabel = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol');

  // 3️⃣ add your layer *before* that label layer
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'my-data-layer',
    type: 'fill',
    source: 'my-data',
    paint: { 'fill-color': '#ff6600', 'fill-opacity': 0.4 }
  }, firstLabel?.id);   // if no label layer exists, it just appends
});
```

That’s it – your GeoJSON will now sit **beneath** the street names and other map labels. Happy mapping! 🚀

