Below is a quick checklist of everything you need to have in place before the OSM raster tiles actually appear on the map.  
The source definition you posted is only half of the story – you still have to **add a layer that uses that source**, and you’ll want a few extra source options (tileSize, maxzoom, attribution, etc.) to keep MapLibre happy and to stay on the right side of the OSM Tile Usage Policy.

---

## 1️⃣  Add the source **and** a raster layer

```js
// 1️⃣  Add the source
map.addSource('osm-raster', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: [
    // OSM’s public tile server (XYZ scheme)
    'https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
  ],
  // Optional but recommended
  tileSize: 256,               // OSM tiles are 256 px
  maxzoom: 19,                 // OSM serves up to z=19
  minzoom: 0,
  attribution: '&copy; <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap</a> contributors'
});

// 2️⃣  Add a raster layer that points at the source
map.addLayer({
  id: 'osm-raster-layer',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'osm-raster',
  // You can limit the zoom range here as well
  minzoom: 0,
  maxzoom: 19,
  // Optional paint properties
  paint: {
    // e.g. make the tiles semi‑transparent while you test
    // 'raster-opacity': 0.9
  }
});
```

> **Why the layer is required** – In MapLibre a *source* only describes where the data lives. A *layer* tells the renderer **how** to draw that data (type, styling, ordering, etc.). Without a layer, the source is never visualised.

---

## 2️⃣  Make sure the map’s style includes the source & layer

If you’re building a full style JSON (instead of calling `addSource`/`addLayer` imperatively), the same structure appears under the `sources` and `layers` arrays:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "osm-raster": {
      "type": "raster",
      "tiles": ["https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"],
      "tileSize": 256,
      "maxzoom": 19,
      "attribution": "&copy; <a href=\"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright\">OpenStreetMap</a> contributors"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "osm-raster-layer",
      "type": "raster",
      "source": "osm-raster",
      "minzoom": 0,
      "maxzoom": 19
    }
  ]
}
```

---

## 3️⃣  Attribution control (optional but strongly recommended)

MapLibre ships with an attribution control that will automatically show the `attribution` string you set on the source. If you disabled the control, re‑enable it:

```js
map.addControl(new maplibregl.AttributionControl({
  compact: true   // optional, makes it collapse on small screens
}));
```

---

## 4️⃣  Respect the OSM Tile Usage Policy

| Policy point | What you should do |
|--------------|-------------------|
| **Rate limits** | Do **not** hammer the public tile server with high‑frequency requests (e.g., > 2 req s⁻¹ from a single IP). Use a cache or a third‑party tile provider for production apps. |
| **User‑Agent / Referer** | OSM’s servers log the `User‑Agent` and `Referer`. If you’re serving tiles from a backend, set a meaningful `User‑Agent` (e.g., `my‑app/1.0 (+https://mydomain.com)`). Browsers already send the page URL as `Referer`. |
| **Attribution** | The `attribution` string above satisfies the requirement. Keep it visible. |
| **Heavy usage** | For high‑traffic sites consider using a commercial tile provider (MapTiler, Thunderforest, etc.) or host your own tile cache (e.g., `tileserver-gl`, `mod_tile`). |

---

## 5️⃣  CORS – No extra work needed for OSM

OpenStreetMap’s public tile server sends the header `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`, so the raster tiles can be fetched from any origin without extra proxying. If you ever switch to a private tile server, make sure it also serves the proper CORS header.

---

## 6️⃣  Optional tweaks you might want

| Setting | Why you might use it |
|---------|----------------------|
| `raster-fade-duration` (paint) | Smooth fade‑in when tiles load (`0` disables the fade). |
| `raster-opacity` (paint) | Blend OSM tiles with another base layer. |
| `raster-resampling` (paint) | Choose `linear` (default) or `nearest` for pixel‑perfect scaling. |
| `bounds` on the source | Limit the source to the world extent (`[-180, -85, 180, 85]`) if you want to avoid unnecessary requests outside the valid area. |
| `scheme: 'xyz'` | Explicitly tells MapLibre you’re using the XYZ scheme (the default for raster sources). |

---

## ✅ TL;DR – Minimal working snippet

```js
map.addSource('osm-raster', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: ['https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 19,
  attribution: '&copy; <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap</a> contributors'
});

map.addLayer({
  id: 'osm-raster-layer',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'osm-raster'
});
```

Add the attribution control (or keep the default) and you’re good to go.  

That’s everything you need for the OSM raster tiles to render correctly in MapLibre. Happy mapping! 🚀

